{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775348255-bFf7MIKiptareQIiudy7","role":"assistant","created":1775348262,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I need to find the yields for:\n- February 1943 (High-grade corporate bonds)\n- February 1944 (High-grade corporate bonds)\n- January 1945 (High-grade corporate bonds)\n\nFrom the 1943 page (page 68):\nLooking at the Monthly Series, I can see:\n- 1943-Jan: 2.70\n- 1943-Feb: 2.68\n\nSo February 1943 = 2.68%\n\nNow from the 1944 page (page 83):\nLooking at the Monthly Series, I need to find February 1944.\n\nScrolling through the data:\n- 1944-Jan: 2.65\n- Looking at the 1944 data continuing...\n\nActually wait, I need to see what the table shows. Looking at the format, it's a continuation table. Let me look more carefully:\n\nLooking at the Monthly Series section in the 1944 bulletin, I can see:\n- For 1944-Jan: Partially tax-exempt = 1.95, Taxable = 2.49, High-grade corporate = 2.65\n\nSo January 1944 = 2.65%\n\nBut I need February 1944. Looking at the weekly and daily series:\n- The weekly series shows Feb 5, 11, 19, 26 with values around 2.65-2.66\n- The daily series shows Feb values around 2.64-2.66\n\nLet me look more carefully... the table shows the monthly series continues with the latest months at the right side. Looking at the structure, I need to find \"Feb\" in the 1944 column.\n\nActually wait, looking at the Monthly Series more carefully:\n- 1943-Feb: appears in the first date column\n- Let me re-read the table\n\nThe table shows multiple date columns in each row. The last column shows the most recent dates.\n\nLooking at row \"1944-Jan\" it shows: 1.95, 2.49, 2.65\n\n\nI'll investigate the monthly data for February and January 1945, noting the lack of a 1945 page and the specific requirement for January 1945.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_e7d53a3ff5344a48ab9fc1b8","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"cat /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_19